    {
      "old": "The rule of the link:/books/symbolic-language/what-is-the-point/[What Is the Point?] chapter cuts both ways: the pointing gets a vote, not a veto — and neither does the re-reading, until the letters and the witnesses agree.",
      "new": "The rule of the link:/books/symbolic-language/what-is-the-point/[What Is the Point?] chapter cuts both ways: the pointing gets a vote, not a veto — and so does the re-reading, until the letters and the witnesses agree.",
      "type": "grammar",
      "severity": "high",
      "summary": "Corrects mismatched “neither does” after an affirmative verb phrase.",
      "rationale": "“Neither does” requires a negative antecedent; “gets a vote, not a veto” is affirmative, so the parallel must be “and so does.”"
    },
